WEBVTT

1
00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:03.760
Perfect.

2
00:30:03.760 --> 00:30:04.080
All right.

3
00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:09.520
Well, thank you all very much
for joining this session of ELO today.

4
00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:10.880
I'm very excited to be here.

5
00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:13.000
My name is Jeremy Andriano.

6
00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:15.680
And my very recently completed M.A.

7
00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:19.000
thesis research was centered around
platform studies— a broad platform

8
00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:23.320
studies inspired comparative
analysis of the digital

9
00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:28.120
writing platforms Twine and Inky,
with a specific interest in the ways

10
00:30:28.120 --> 00:30:32.000
that they can be used in creative
writing classrooms that kind of foreground

11
00:30:32.960 --> 00:30:36.160
literary, aesthetic,
or rhetorical uses of the hyperlink

12
00:30:36.200 --> 00:30:39.280
and downplay computer
and technical aspects.

13
00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:42.400
And I should say, even though this is ELO

14
00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:45.960
(un)supervised, this research
was very well supervised by Dr.

15
00:30:45.960 --> 00:30:48.000
Jason Boyd.
Thank you for being here today.

16
00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:51.040
And I'd also like to thank ComCult’s
faculty.

17
00:30:51.040 --> 00:30:54.160
And just,
I was honored to call my defense chair.

18
00:30:54.160 --> 00:30:56.960
And just an endless source of

19
00:30:58.520 --> 00:30:59.320
hope and encouragement.

20
00:30:59.320 --> 00:31:02.040
Monique Tschofen is also here. So
thank you.

21
00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:05.120
But I think I can move pretty quickly.

22
00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:10.360
I know most of you or all of you
are aware of each of these tools.

23
00:31:10.720 --> 00:31:14.320
They're both digital authoring platforms
for writing interactive hypertext,

24
00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:15.920
and they're both great for use

25
00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:18.960
in the classroom for teaching students
who are new to this kind of writing

26
00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:20.880
for a lot of the same reasons,
like the qualities

27
00:31:20.880 --> 00:31:25.240
that they have in common, make them great
for beginner and classroom use.

28
00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:29.280
They've both demonstrated a dedication
to remaining open source ish

29
00:31:29.320 --> 00:31:33.160
and being freely available,
and they do so through different methods

30
00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:34.520
and for slightly different reasons.

31
00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:36.800
But they have that in common.

32
00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:40.800
They both make use of a very easy
to grasp markup language for creating

33
00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:45.760
and structuring hypertext, for creating
the nodes and linking them together.

34
00:31:46.080 --> 00:31:49.640
That can be grasped within
a single session, but they're both capable

35
00:31:49.640 --> 00:31:53.760
of layering on complex programing logic,
and they both have ample

36
00:31:53.800 --> 00:31:58.640
tutorial material available for students
to expand and learn that as they go.

37
00:31:58.680 --> 00:32:02.000
That's not something I would recommend
starting with in the classroom.

38
00:32:02.320 --> 00:32:06.160
And lastly, they both publish
to sort of a default appearance.

39
00:32:06.160 --> 00:32:08.520
They're easily shareable as HTML.

40
00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:13.640
Writers don't have to do any additional
formatting or HTML configuring in order

41
00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:16.240
to make a playable version
and share it with other people.

42
00:32:16.240 --> 00:32:19.440
So new writers can do this quickly.

43
00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:23.120
But for all these similarities,
these platforms are really—

44
00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:24.960
they're quite distinct.

45
00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:28.240
They both let writers
use hyperlinks to make meaning in creative

46
00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:32.320
and expressive ways, but they do
so through very different methods.

47
00:32:32.360 --> 00:32:35.120
And this creates advantages
and disadvantages

48
00:32:35.120 --> 00:32:37.720
depending on what the writer
is trying to accomplish.

49
00:32:37.720 --> 00:32:41.680
What Skains refers to as key creative
capacities of each of these platforms.

50
00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:45.200
And with Twine and Inky,
I find that these differences

51
00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:48.280
can actually be really useful
in demonstrating,

52
00:32:48.320 --> 00:32:51.480
especially for new students,
some really fundamental concepts

53
00:32:51.480 --> 00:32:53.320
for writing interactive literature,

54
00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:57.080
but then also these different approaches
taken by each of these platforms.

55
00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:00.920
They align with some interactive genres
better than others.

56
00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:03.960
They accommodate different writing styles
and different learning styles.

57
00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:08.680
So, you know, like educators,
like Lyle Skains and Amy Letter

58
00:33:08.680 --> 00:33:12.040
and others, you know, they stress
the teaching technology is hard.

59
00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:13.600
We shouldn't be teaching the technology.

60
00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:15.000
And I'm not suggesting that students

61
00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:18.000
or even instructors
need to learn both of these platforms,

62
00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:21.640
but having a basic understanding
of both of them can help decide where

63
00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:24.120
to expend your energy,
and it can help you direct your students

64
00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:27.760
as to where they might want to spend
their energy for any given project.

65
00:33:27.800 --> 00:33:32.200
And this is most apparent
when we look at Marie-Laure

66
00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:35.400
Ryan's several interpretive modes
or metaphors,

67
00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:41.000
or the ways hyperlinks can be used to
represent different things for the reader,

68
00:33:41.040 --> 00:33:45.320
giving more meaning than simply advancing
a page or clicking through a database.

69
00:33:45.320 --> 00:33:48.560
And one of the most prominent modes
in use is,

70
00:33:48.600 --> 00:33:52.520
of course, the spatial metaphor
in which the text is a territory

71
00:33:52.520 --> 00:33:55.200
and the links are roads
between those passages.

72
00:33:55.200 --> 00:33:56.480
And those passages

73
00:33:57.720 --> 00:33:59.320
can be representative of different things.

74
00:33:59.320 --> 00:34:00.880
They can be locations in a story.

75
00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:04.640
They can be topics of conversation
or subjects between two characters.

76
00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:08.000
Or they might
just be the parts of an academic essay.

77
00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:12.800
But at its very foundation, Twine embodies
the spatial conceptualization

78
00:34:12.800 --> 00:34:17.000
of hypertext, and its entire interface
is designed to let the writer

79
00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:20.160
visualize the passages
in spatial relation to one another.

80
00:34:21.160 --> 00:34:25.640
This is demonstrated perfectly
by Porpetine’s “Howling Dogs,” which

81
00:34:25.640 --> 00:34:29.600
demonstrates Twine's natural alignment
with the spatial modes of hypertext.

82
00:34:29.680 --> 00:34:33.520
We see on the left the famous hub
passage, the room of dark metal.

83
00:34:33.640 --> 00:34:35.400
It's focused almost exclusively

84
00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:38.960
on the spatial relationship
between the objects and other rooms,

85
00:34:39.160 --> 00:34:41.000
the links
that are spread throughout the text,

86
00:34:41.000 --> 00:34:44.760
and they invite the reader to explore
those spaces, to kind of probe and poke.

87
00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:47.600
And then on the right,
I've put together just a simple mock

88
00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:50.040
up of what
the view of this passage might look like,

89
00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:53.600
with the passages arranged
according to their described positions.

90
00:34:54.640 --> 00:34:55.800
But this—

91
00:34:55.800 --> 00:34:59.640
Aaron Reed in “50 Years of Text
Games” puts together this great

92
00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:04.000
visual map of “Howling Dogs,” and
I don't want to spend too much time on it.

93
00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:07.000
But I think many of you actually know
this story

94
00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:10.120
intimately.

95
00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:12.960
I researched a lot of— So.

96
00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:17.800
But basically we see the room of dark
metal starts here, and then the reader

97
00:35:18.120 --> 00:35:19.400
shuffles through the activity room,

98
00:35:19.400 --> 00:35:23.280
and then the story is told across
five vignettes.

99
00:35:23.280 --> 00:35:27.600
And as Reed points out,
the last vignette, Empress,

100
00:35:27.640 --> 00:35:30.800
you can really kind of see the long linear
path that it takes.

101
00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:34.600
And if the reader,
when the reader finally pokes

102
00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:38.360
and probes the right ways, you end up
at the true ending of the story.

103
00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:41.600
But there's all these potential pitfalls
that take you back to the beginning.

104
00:35:45.800 --> 00:35:48.320
This just demonstrates
how spatial “Howling Dogs” is.

105
00:35:48.320 --> 00:35:48.960
It really is.

106
00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:49.600
Like,

107
00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:53.400
for those of you who haven't read it,
it's a fantastic experience of it really.

108
00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:57.640
You are feeling your way in the dark and
it is encouraging you to find a way out.

109
00:35:57.640 --> 00:36:00.640
And it's a beautiful experience
when it works.

110
00:36:01.880 --> 00:36:04.880
Perhaps a better way
to kind of demonstrate “Howling Dogs”’s

111
00:36:04.880 --> 00:36:07.920
close relationship with the Twine platform
and its dependance

112
00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:11.880
on spatial interpretations of hyperlinks
though, is to think about writing it

113
00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:12.920
using a different tool.

114
00:36:12.920 --> 00:36:14.960
So this is what the room of dark

115
00:36:14.960 --> 00:36:17.960
metal might look like
if it were created with Inky.

116
00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:21.760
We'll go into this in a bit more detail,
but when you write with Inky, you write in

117
00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:26.800
lines, and ordered lines that are read in
what an Ink story thinks of as its flow.

118
00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:31.200
And here you can create passages,
and this is what it would look like

119
00:36:31.200 --> 00:36:34.280
to create links that lead
to different passages that then you could,

120
00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:37.680
further down in the script, describe
the text of each of these passages.

121
00:36:37.680 --> 00:36:41.120
And this is what Inky’s
default presentation would look like

122
00:36:41.120 --> 00:36:42.120
to the reader.

123
00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:48.200
And the big thing it misses here
is there's no natural way to incorporate

124
00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:52.680
the links within the text in an Ink story
the way you can in a Twine story.

125
00:36:53.280 --> 00:36:56.120
So again,
you could create the similar effect

126
00:36:56.120 --> 00:37:01.040
and go to the different nodes,
but you lose that kind of key element.

127
00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:01.960
To what?

128
00:37:03.480 --> 00:37:05.760
To what makes “Howling Dogs” what it is.

129
00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:09.680
And this is because when you're writing
with Inky, Inky aligns better with what

130
00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:13.440
Marie-Laure Ryan thinks of as the theater
or the performative interpretation

131
00:37:13.440 --> 00:37:14.200
of hypertext.

132
00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:17.960
Or I think of it as almost a temporal
thought of hypertext,

133
00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:22.600
where you're moving through a story,
a linear plot in order with branches.

134
00:37:23.120 --> 00:37:25.520
As we see here, Ryan compares
this kind of hypertext

135
00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:29.040
to a stage performance, like a script
that's being read that occasionally asks

136
00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:33.200
the reader to make decisions about
how the performance is going to proceed.

137
00:37:33.240 --> 00:37:36.880
So let me see— on the left here,
as I said, it's written as numbered lines.

138
00:37:37.320 --> 00:37:41.720
You introduce choices
for the reader that then appear.

139
00:37:42.040 --> 00:37:46.520
You test over here, and then what happens
if each of the choices are selected—

140
00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:48.000
is filled in underneath?

141
00:37:49.240 --> 00:37:51.240
Again, I don't
want to go too far into technique here,

142
00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:55.400
but it is a very different writing process
than thinking spatially within Twine.

143
00:37:55.640 --> 00:37:59.600
And this is again personified
by kind of a foundational work.

144
00:37:59.640 --> 00:38:02.960
The Ink scripting language
was essentially developed as a development

145
00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:06.200
tool while Inkle Studios
was creating “80 Days.”

146
00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:09.680
So it makes perfect sense
that they're intrinsically tied.

147
00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:13.520
Ink is designed to write stories
that are presented like stories,

148
00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:17.400
which are downstream branching narratives
that move in one direction

149
00:38:17.400 --> 00:38:20.640
and allow the reader
to make selections of text

150
00:38:20.680 --> 00:38:24.080
that then gets added and read
into that performance of the text.

151
00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:25.920
And so we just hear some screenshots

152
00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:29.280
of the way, like dialog
choices are presented to the reader.

153
00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:33.960
Here again,
Aaron Reed dedicates his 2014 entry

154
00:38:34.040 --> 00:38:38.880
in “50 Years of Text Games”
to “80 Days” and showcases here.

155
00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:41.960
So this flowchart,
this is very different than the one

156
00:38:41.960 --> 00:38:45.360
for “Howling Dogs,”
which is kind of meant to be explored.

157
00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:48.360
And it's like feeling around walls almost.

158
00:38:48.400 --> 00:38:52.360
Ink stories move in one direction
in this case, you know, travel

159
00:38:52.400 --> 00:38:53.880
literally from east to west.

160
00:38:53.880 --> 00:38:57.080
And, you know, you can see where like
it's like a flowchart.

161
00:38:57.120 --> 00:38:58.240
It branches differently.

162
00:38:58.240 --> 00:39:01.840
It's not meant to be— you're not meant
to backtrack in a single reading.

163
00:39:01.880 --> 00:39:05.000
You make one decision
and you close off other decisions.

164
00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:08.200
And it's interesting.

165
00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:12.160
And Reed points
this out here, that when decided to adapt

166
00:39:12.160 --> 00:39:14.680
Jules Verne's
“Around the World in 80 Days,”

167
00:39:14.680 --> 00:39:17.800
like they chose that plot
because it matched this style

168
00:39:17.800 --> 00:39:19.840
of storytelling
and not the other way around.

169
00:39:19.840 --> 00:39:23.640
Like they knew they wanted to tell a
downstream flowchart, branching narrative.

170
00:39:23.640 --> 00:39:26.800
And they found that because “80 Days”
you have to travel east.

171
00:39:26.880 --> 00:39:27.800
This worked perfectly.

172
00:39:27.800 --> 00:39:29.800
And I just think that really shows
great synergy

173
00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:33.080
and alignment between all these things
like the software,

174
00:39:33.120 --> 00:39:36.120
the story they're trying to tell,
and then the way they tell it.

175
00:39:36.600 --> 00:39:38.120
I'm going to move a little quickly here.

176
00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:41.120
But this is, you know, if

177
00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:46.760
being able to spread links around
in making an explorable screen is Twine’s

178
00:39:46.760 --> 00:39:49.160
bread-and-butter, Ink’s
bread-and-butter is being able

179
00:39:49.160 --> 00:39:52.240
to let the reader select
the text that then gets added.

180
00:39:52.280 --> 00:39:56.600
It creates this back and forth
collaborative text generating

181
00:39:56.640 --> 00:39:59.680
kind of sensation
for between author and reader.

182
00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:02.680
And so again,
this is a close up of “80 Days.”

183
00:40:02.680 --> 00:40:04.400
You have like three options
of the next line.

184
00:40:04.400 --> 00:40:07.240
That can be
when the reader selects the first one.

185
00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:11.560
You see that text is read
right into the plot or into the display.

186
00:40:11.640 --> 00:40:15.120
The other two choices go away,
and then there's even more text

187
00:40:15.160 --> 00:40:19.160
that the reader didn't know was going
to be there, and it leads to the next one.

188
00:40:19.160 --> 00:40:20.040
And it's a

189
00:40:21.440 --> 00:40:22.320
constant process

190
00:40:22.320 --> 00:40:26.720
of co-creation of the manuscript
when you're playing through “80 Days.”

191
00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:30.640
And again,
I'm not going to go into too much detail.

192
00:40:30.640 --> 00:40:33.720
I think a lot of you know how to do this
better than I do myself, but

193
00:40:33.720 --> 00:40:37.600
this is a demonstration of how like, it's
the simplest, most elegant mechanic

194
00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:41.480
that Inky allows the Ink
scripting language and by— you know,

195
00:40:41.680 --> 00:40:46.520
by extension, its editing software, Inky
allows the writer to come up with this.

196
00:40:46.520 --> 00:40:47.480
And this demonstration

197
00:40:47.480 --> 00:40:51.080
just shows how you can keep
your dialog punctuation consistent.

198
00:40:51.360 --> 00:40:54.960
Anything that appears before the brackets
here will be in the choice,

199
00:40:54.960 --> 00:40:57.000
and also in the resulting text.

200
00:40:57.000 --> 00:41:00.960
What's in the brackets will only appear
in the choices as the link is there,

201
00:41:00.960 --> 00:41:04.040
and then everything afterwards is
what appears after the reader

202
00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:05.560
makes that selection. So

203
00:41:07.040 --> 00:41:08.680
in practice it looks like this.

204
00:41:08.680 --> 00:41:10.640
You know, like, no,
I couldn't eat another bite.

205
00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:12.640
This is punctuated correctly.

206
00:41:12.640 --> 00:41:16.680
And then here we see if that's selected,
it turns into the appropriate comma,

207
00:41:16.720 --> 00:41:20.360
then the dialog brackets
and then finishes with the attribution.

208
00:41:20.720 --> 00:41:25.200
This is just a really specific example to
kind of show like I can't express enough.

209
00:41:25.200 --> 00:41:27.600
I didn't go through it
to even try and demonstrate it.

210
00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:32.080
But doing this is— it involves a lot
more work if you're doing it within Twine.

211
00:41:32.080 --> 00:41:35.640
Certainly not that it's impossible,
but it's cumbersome.

212
00:41:35.680 --> 00:41:40.440
And this is what Inky was designed to do,
and Twine was not.

213
00:41:40.440 --> 00:41:42.000
So that makes sense.

214
00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:45.480
So again,
this whole presentation is kind of

215
00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:48.520
to point out that there's
lots of these kind of examples where

216
00:41:48.520 --> 00:41:50.400
if you're helping students
learn about hypertext

217
00:41:50.400 --> 00:41:53.640
for the first time, it can be helpful
to know if they want to write—

218
00:41:53.680 --> 00:41:56.280
And you can tell them it's helpful
to know up front if they want to write

219
00:41:56.280 --> 00:41:58.960
branching dialog trees,
or if they want to write

220
00:41:58.960 --> 00:42:02.240
if style explorations like go north,
go east, go west— like that

221
00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:05.440
will impact
which platform they should use.

222
00:42:05.480 --> 00:42:06.000
And you know,

223
00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:08.160
they can use it
just while they work on that project

224
00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:10.680
and learn something else
for a different project.

225
00:42:10.680 --> 00:42:14.000
And it also might just appeal
to different students in different ways.

226
00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:17.320
Some people might want to structure
their hypertext graphically in Twine

227
00:42:17.720 --> 00:42:19.440
and then fill things in, and others might

228
00:42:19.440 --> 00:42:22.920
just like to free write prose,
which I think Inky works better for.

229
00:42:22.960 --> 00:42:26.240
And it's just those kind of things
I kind of want to focus on.

230
00:42:26.280 --> 00:42:29.040
It's really what I came to.
I think it's really fascinating.

231
00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:31.640
And, you know, frankly, I

232
